# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Expo app configuration
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Copy to `.env`:   cp .env.example .env
#
# EXPO_PUBLIC_* values are inlined into the app bundle at build time, so only
# put public configuration here. Firebase web config IS public by design —
# access is controlled by Firebase security rules and authorised domains, not
# by hiding these values. Never put a Twilio auth token in this file; that
# belongs in server/.env, which never reaches the app.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- Firebase Phone Auth (primary provider) --------------------------------
# Firebase console -> Project settings -> General -> Your apps -> Web app,
# then copy the values out of the `firebaseConfig` object it shows you.
#
# Also required, in Authentication -> Sign-in method:
#   * enable the "Phone" provider
#   * to test without the paid Blaze plan, add a number under
#     "Phone numbers for testing" with a fixed 6-digit code
#
# Leave these blank to fall back to the Express server (Twilio / demo).
EXPO_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_API_KEY=
EXPO_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN=
EXPO_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=
EXPO_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_STORAGE_BUCKET=
EXPO_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_MESSAGING_SENDER_ID=
EXPO_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_APP_ID=

# --- Verification server (fallback provider) -------------------------------
# Used when Firebase is not configured, and always in Expo Go, where Firebase
# phone auth cannot run (its reCAPTCHA step needs a browser).
#
# Found automatically by default:
#   Expo Go on a phone  -> http://<your-pc-LAN-ip>:4000
#   Android emulator    -> http://10.0.2.2:4000
#   iOS simulator / web -> http://localhost:4000
# Set this only when the server runs somewhere else (a tunnel or deployed host).
# EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL=https://your-server.example.com
